  'Introduction to the Humanities',
  'Introduction to the Humanities is a first-year course in the study of human meaning, expression, memory, value, and culture. Students read literature, examine philosophy, analyze visual art and architecture, listen to music, interpret performance, study history and religion, and investigate how language, media, power, and identity shape cultural life. The course emphasizes close reading, close looking, historical context, ethical reasoning, cross-cultural comparison, research literacy, and public communication.',
  $ihumjson${"slug": "introduction-to-the-humanities", "title": "Introduction to the Humanities", "description": "Introduction to the Humanities is a first-year course in the study of human meaning, expression, memory, value, and culture. Students read literature, examine philosophy, analyze visual art and architecture, listen to music, interpret performance, study history and religion, and investigate how language, media, power, and identity shape cultural life. The course emphasizes close reading, close looking, historical context, ethical reasoning, cross-cultural comparison, research literacy, and public communication.", "topics": [{"id": "caa984b6-fa9b-45d4-852d-5e2eeb5221b3", "type": "topic", "title": "What Are the Humanities? Questions, Methods, and Human Meaning", "description": "This opening unit defines the humanities as disciplines that study meaning, value, expression, memory, and human experience. Students learn why literature, philosophy, art, history, religion, music, performance, and language remain essential for interpreting culture and modern life.", "children": [{"id": "2946eb4f-27d6-4347-bdfa-f245b7e528c6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "The scope of humanistic study", "children": [{"id": "9645575b-ab00-4ce8-949a-d10e011bbfa1", "type": "concept", "title": "Meaning, value, interpretation, and form"}, {"id": "59bd6aa9-0d39-49b5-bf9e-7297ae6e2052", "type": "concept", "title": "Texts, artifacts, performances, images, and places"}, {"id": "0ac45513-10f3-4edb-9de2-f2c60a7c9715", "type": "concept", "title": "Humanistic questions about identity, justice, mortality, beauty, and belonging"}, {"id": "6d82bdea-3f04-494e-acba-9d03ff944a8b", "type": "concept", "title": "Connections to liberal arts education"}], "description": "Students learn what distinguishes humanities inquiry from scientific and social-scientific explanation while also seeing where the fields overlap."}, {"id": "1c6a7ed1-ab99-47d0-aabf-433461b5ff4c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Humanities as methods of attention", "children": [{"id": "c8598667-50fc-4701-bf9f-15ba4cdde364", "type": "concept", "title": "Slow reading and close looking"}, {"id": "437c2c62-76e7-435a-8a69-fed610cf845c", "type": "concept", "title": "Description before interpretation"}, {"id": "9199d2f9-4d3d-4a4e-a677-6ce2e47f6d3c", "type": "concept", "title": "Historical and cultural framing"}, {"id": "33ce7cea-7237-44d9-9bac-4ad12ba8b0f7", "type": "concept", "title": "Ambiguity, evidence, and multiple meanings"}], "description": "The point is to cultivate careful noticing before judgment: reading, listening, looking, comparing, and contextualizing."}, {"id": "bfc70754-f2b9-485c-ba06-1b78ea5e4397", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Why the humanities matter now", "children": [{"id": "f356dcfb-34a4-428c-8aa1-2e9607fd8b7d", "type": "concept", "title": "Citizenship and civic imagination"}, {"id": "50aca8ef-3a8f-442f-9f69-23978f121e97", "type": "concept", "title": "Empathy and perspective-taking"}, {"id": "4d3f3272-4fb4-4368-a073-5053c4a676f1", "type": "concept", "title": "Cultural memory and public debate"}, {"id": "42f21ee4-d633-4aae-adbf-f994d8920d86", "type": "concept", "title": "Humanistic skills in contemporary careers"}], "description": "Students connect the humanities to public life, democratic dialogue, ethical judgment, creativity, and self-understanding."}]}, {"id": "34c03ea5-1517-48cf-bd1e-3428030acd83", "type": "topic", "title": "Humanistic Inquiry: Close Reading, Interpretation, Context, and Argument", "description": "This unit gives students the toolkit for doing humanities work. They practice turning observation into interpretation, interpretation into argument, and argument into clear academic writing.", "children": [{"id": "26546832-b1c8-4981-9875-4e9e67ba3d85", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Close reading and close looking", "children": [{"id": "b5b2ab73-79a4-4c76-925c-e3a744977b20", "type": "concept", "title": "Diction, image, metaphor, structure, and genre"}, {"id": "8be17831-8017-454c-8224-eb657bcd8eb8", "type": "concept", "title": "Composition, line, color, material, and scale"}, {"id": "2f804f09-32cf-45e6-810b-dc450e38f005", "type": "concept", "title": "Sound, rhythm, gesture, and performance detail"}, {"id": "d9877ae5-8ae2-4f5d-b85b-81c27e8a38ca", "type": "concept", "title": "Patterns, tensions, and anomalies"}], "description": "Students learn to build interpretations from specific evidence rather than broad impressions."}, {"id": "975177f2-a165-450b-8d50-f4b16cd050b2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Context and historical method", "children": [{"id": "57f585e6-88ce-4fed-9fcb-87401691ad0f", "type": "concept", "title": "Author, maker, patron, and audience"}, {"id": "56a9604b-df12-466a-a4e3-ed858f5e0402", "type": "concept", "title": "Period, place, movement, and medium"}, {"id": "73b21331-9f7f-4150-b2f9-00bb9f4acbe2", "type": "concept", "title": "Reception and changing interpretation"}, {"id": "b1b9ae3e-0291-4448-abb4-34843eab9636", "type": "concept", "title": "Archives and primary sources"}], "description": "Students learn that works are made inside worlds: institutions, languages, audiences, technologies, and power relations."}, {"id": "ad3baee6-ef01-44e6-9c87-26721a43d08c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Argument and scholarly conversation", "children": [{"id": "02396f8a-074d-4e47-8fde-31d51ef72dee", "type": "concept", "title": "Research questions and thesis statements"}, {"id": "2e0bde40-b1fa-47c9-80f9-ef6fbc01db7e", "type": "concept", "title": "Evidence selection and explanation"}, {"id": "187014fc-b7f0-4258-ad4f-1d7cb26d5381", "type": "concept", "title": "Counterargument and revision"}, {"id": "38c150d8-e19c-4dbc-b761-faf45cda182f", "type": "concept", "title": "Citation and academic integrity"}], "description": "The gist is learning to enter a debate responsibly, with claims, evidence, warrants, and respectful disagreement."}]}, {"id": "21b5e6e6-5fdb-4b9e-9bf3-2f3507a1cd51", "type": "topic", "title": "Literature as World-Making: Poetry, Narrative, Drama, and Myth", "description": "Literature gives students practice interpreting language as art, memory, thought, and social imagination. The unit treats literary works as crafted forms that build worlds, voices, conflicts, and ethical possibilities.", "children": [{"id": "08ca7da2-eafb-453c-9483-6585747b88bb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Poetry and compressed language", "children": [{"id": "294fc7bf-fbef-4111-827c-35c492bfc6c4", "type": "concept", "title": "Speaker, tone, and address"}, {"id": "b4a47046-d42b-4da0-9cd7-22568efdc048", "type": "concept", "title": "Figurative language and imagery"}, {"id": "a27a7b2f-671c-4e71-b4c9-d879b45313c7", "type": "concept", "title": "Form, meter, line, and stanza"}, {"id": "43d28f2a-4ac5-4f95-8353-ecf66af51039", "type": "concept", "title": "Translation and performance"}], "description": "Students learn how poems make meaning through rhythm, image, sound, form, and silence."}, {"id": "7c368f92-49c5-4579-a2af-95ca954477df", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Narrative and fiction", "children": [{"id": "1aa7c6f4-babd-4a02-985c-41cea44fe648", "type": "concept", "title": "Plot, scene, and structure"}, {"id": "6839bc09-45c0-4e2a-85b0-69473b03f895", "type": "concept", "title": "Narrator and point of view"}, {"id": "c674ceef-4f98-42ce-8d4c-a17252e188e9", "type": "concept", "title": "Character, desire, and conflict"}, {"id": "293af96e-e70e-47e8-b3bc-0ba2e9a4c325", "type": "concept", "title": "World-building and social context"}], "description": "Students examine how stories organize time, causality, character, perspective, and moral complexity."}, {"id": "800f5096-b836-4d71-90a7-ddc9c1d4b4a5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Drama, myth, and collective imagination", "children": [{"id": "267631e5-6636-43c8-b37a-b1ce044bd062", "type": "concept", "title": "Tragedy, comedy, and ritual"}, {"id": "8f1c4d16-b897-4b36-b51f-63ef8a7695d8", "type": "concept", "title": "Mythic patterns and archetypes"}, {"id": "f548ff5a-a29b-4dbf-8d60-763a95c5e1a7", "type": "concept", "title": "Performance, audience, and embodiment"}, {"id": "b81a2ef6-60b4-4abd-8349-bc21e6b86fbd", "type": "concept", "title": "Adaptation and reinterpretation"}], "description": "Students explore texts meant for performance and stories that communities use to explain origins, values, and crisis."}]}, {"id": "22fa60a8-4963-419d-bdb3-bc568c0132f8", "type": "topic", "title": "Philosophy and the History of Ideas: Reality, Knowledge, Ethics, and the Self", "description": "Philosophy teaches students to ask precise questions, test assumptions, and evaluate reasons. This unit introduces major philosophical problems that shape humanistic inquiry across periods and cultures.", "children": [{"id": "67a656ea-5d41-46fe-bd0b-6476abf48a05", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reality, knowledge, and skepticism", "children": [{"id": "5b1bef6c-3469-4f59-9851-0d637fff3e9d", "type": "concept", "title": "Metaphysics and being"}, {"id": "fc072107-efec-440e-b5d7-e6649d693f74", "type": "concept", "title": "Epistemology and evidence"}, {"id": "ad432ec5-9223-4412-abb4-5409fc7f770f", "type": "concept", "title": "Skepticism and certainty"}, {"id": "2e934095-de01-42b0-8202-1f71dad74cc7", "type": "concept", "title": "Mind, body, and self"}], "description": "Students learn how philosophers question what exists, what can be known, and how belief can be justified."}, {"id": "9a3560ab-c6a4-470b-b7a1-4b8db7bfc1c8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ethics and moral reasoning", "children": [{"id": "097d414b-5968-47e0-a72a-3eacc79d396a", "type": "concept", "title": "Virtue ethics"}, {"id": "1587b7af-1375-43e3-b7b7-47aec1c18745", "type": "concept", "title": "Deontology and duty"}, {"id": "e9090a61-2a46-44d2-9553-28686fbef709", "type": "concept", "title": "Consequentialism"}, {"id": "af66f385-490d-4a9a-aa0a-4a3259fec359", "type": "concept", "title": "Care, responsibility, and applied ethics"}], "description": "Students practice comparing frameworks for judgment and applying them to difficult human situations."}, {"id": "05654193-24e9-43c0-9fde-66bbb0a95e07", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Political philosophy and justice", "children": [{"id": "14ee292a-4f47-4e61-aa90-69081f69ad64", "type": "concept", "title": "Liberty, equality, and rights"}, {"id": "9efda216-830c-4a59-8b64-0566e9d99d6d", "type": "concept", "title": "Authority and legitimacy"}, {"id": "8eea75f8-a34d-4341-9358-11a17171cf48", "type": "concept", "title": "Justice and distribution"}, {"id": "f2a5bdec-ecc7-4a98-a215-e80dec6a48e1", "type": "concept", "title": "Democracy, citizenship, and dissent"}], "description": "The unit connects ideas about the good life to institutions, rights, authority, and public life."}]}, {"id": "e4d88fd6-a22e-4137-8b3d-9d3061f21426", "type": "topic", "title": "Visual Arts and Architecture: Seeing, Form, Space, and Power", "description": "This unit trains visual literacy. Students learn to interpret images, objects, buildings, and spaces as cultural arguments shaped by materials, techniques, patrons, audiences, and histories.", "children": [{"id": "8965745d-b48a-4c01-bd3c-b2af2995ffae", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Formal visual analysis", "children": [{"id": "dde9130c-9a38-4ff8-bdf5-01e0ccc0411e", "type": "concept", "title": "Line, color, light, texture, and composition"}, {"id": "ad2356a5-16a1-411b-859c-10321d89717d", "type": "concept", "title": "Scale, proportion, balance, and movement"}, {"id": "7d7955ef-83df-4f9d-b893-b671c66d1295", "type": "concept", "title": "Medium, material, and technique"}, {"id": "4b9e01ea-3d33-4cff-91bf-9370e357a56f", "type": "concept", "title": "Iconography and symbolism"}], "description": "Students learn to describe what they see with precision before moving into meaning."}, {"id": "0b3d44e7-c406-4b5a-87a6-80d42e76388c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Architecture and built environments", "children": [{"id": "7696e6e0-7714-4c6a-8232-560f7565350a", "type": "concept", "title": "Function and form"}, {"id": "e9f570ad-718b-4533-9179-abf97468ef04", "type": "concept", "title": "Sacred, civic, domestic, and commercial spaces"}, {"id": "7d613167-6ff9-4276-a682-e638208a2e27", "type": "concept", "title": "Urbanism and landscape"}, {"id": "db3b9c0c-4842-4649-932d-3a825db5841e", "type": "concept", "title": "Monuments, memorials, and contested space"}], "description": "Students examine how space organizes social life, ritual, memory, authority, and belonging."}, {"id": "b96ffe8d-2872-4158-83ea-7bda3fb86a68", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Museums, display, and cultural power", "children": [{"id": "657d291e-c3e0-4c2b-a13e-992ed4428963", "type": "concept", "title": "Collection and provenance"}, {"id": "39f85171-2e18-4e5c-91e1-bb9746725798", "type": "concept", "title": "Curatorial framing"}, {"id": "239a5d28-8399-4110-8253-1c51210cd40e", "type": "concept", "title": "Repatriation and cultural property"}, {"id": "7a9038ed-ee07-48e6-ba8f-ff7b72ddbc94", "type": "concept", "title": "Public access and interpretation"}], "description": "The unit asks how art becomes heritage, who interprets it, and what responsibilities follow."}]}, {"id": "799fb55c-279d-4087-81f9-21d04583e381", "type": "topic", "title": "Music, Theater, Performance, and Embodied Culture", "description": "Humanities study is not only textual or visual. This unit examines live, embodied, and sonic forms that create meaning through voice, movement, time, audience, and ritual.", "children": [{"id": "30eb67e6-bffa-4598-857a-a7c63e7e2685", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Music and listening", "children": [{"id": "e4239f69-e8e5-45d6-bee1-03c8ea93e0f0", "type": "concept", "title": "Melody, rhythm, harmony, timbre, and texture"}, {"id": "bccc3868-d209-4bd1-b996-cc96096d24e4", "type": "concept", "title": "Genre and performance context"}, {"id": "3325ba59-c126-4fac-8b85-06c99bae7e12", "type": "concept", "title": "Sacred, courtly, popular, and experimental music"}, {"id": "01623dbd-4391-4afa-9a34-49e7d714cf90", "type": "concept", "title": "Listening journals and sonic evidence"}], "description": "Students learn to discuss music as patterned sound with cultural, emotional, and historical force."}, {"id": "df4e3748-580b-4ae9-b689-16e000882901", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Theater and performance", "children": [{"id": "a9b13adf-f0f8-46f3-9b18-d9c69ef58dab", "type": "concept", "title": "Script, staging, and dramaturgy"}, {"id": "4f711112-39a2-4cb0-a5e5-e1a5a50704bf", "type": "concept", "title": "Gesture, voice, costume, and set"}, {"id": "5f4e7546-fa96-48d0-8436-1905aabe8925", "type": "concept", "title": "Audience and liveness"}, {"id": "7a742e9f-014a-497b-928c-32b63f8f01f0", "type": "concept", "title": "Performance traditions across cultures"}], "description": "Students study performance as an event made by bodies, spaces, texts, audiences, and conventions."}, {"id": "f8e6a5a6-730c-48b5-a57e-b649a5c1a59a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Embodiment and cultural practice", "children": [{"id": "be8fa72e-a616-4914-96f7-9a8bb5ed93d7", "type": "concept", "title": "Ritual and ceremony"}, {"id": "10e58992-7876-4bfa-b903-b9df5a874b82", "type": "concept", "title": "Dance and movement"}, {"id": "a8c0b07f-131c-4f02-976e-3d55b607967c", "type": "concept", "title": "Everyday performance and identity"}, {"id": "5e376d9b-37d4-421f-bc35-e36ab9106ee9", "type": "concept", "title": "Documentation and performance archives"}], "description": "The goal is to recognize that culture is enacted, repeated, improvised, and contested through bodies."}]}, {"id": "63ae2170-eb7c-4eb3-92ff-221966c089e1", "type": "topic", "title": "History, Memory, Archives, and Cultural Context", "description": "History gives humanities students a disciplined way to study change over time. This unit distinguishes memory from evidence, narrative from archive, and interpretation from mere chronology.", "children": [{"id": "632da7e2-74b2-43ab-9661-73c00ada3369", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Historical thinking", "children": [{"id": "0028af8f-dd0b-4030-af15-1d63d168add5", "type": "concept", "title": "Chronology and periodization"}, {"id": "2248a9c3-bdd5-4f7e-9f54-459d22d4b8ff", "type": "concept", "title": "Cause, consequence, and contingency"}, {"id": "6ad5aaee-2073-4161-a314-2a1113db0bbd", "type": "concept", "title": "Comparison across places and periods"}, {"id": "b2507c7d-66c0-48ad-8e5b-a38b7cd567f0", "type": "concept", "title": "Microhistory and global history"}], "description": "Students learn to analyze causation, contingency, continuity, change, and periodization."}, {"id": "da7efa1c-cd81-45ed-a2fe-3342ba4c1691", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Archives and primary sources", "children": [{"id": "4a4bc01e-f1cd-494e-9e43-76410928df1e", "type": "concept", "title": "Manuscripts, letters, images, maps, and objects"}, {"id": "1d881805-96d1-4b2f-8d1c-28621c0201eb", "type": "concept", "title": "Source criticism and bias"}, {"id": "fadb71c5-b517-4972-bc6d-f1beb81a35ce", "type": "concept", "title": "Silences in the archive"}, {"id": "14223d27-4984-439a-9727-698f0e9ab462", "type": "concept", "title": "Oral history and lived memory"}], "description": "Students practice reading historical evidence as partial, situated, and often contested."}, {"id": "aee17fe5-bc3c-40f0-ac5e-0eb7b1db1a5a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Memory, commemoration, and public history", "children": [{"id": "410db3ff-8ab6-4ede-90b6-88db2bedb539", "type": "concept", "title": "Monuments and memorials"}, {"id": "e4091460-dee6-48b3-b4cf-4a9ac94d4595", "type": "concept", "title": "Museums and heritage sites"}, {"id": "8df803b5-8ced-48f3-b46d-87153f3a7be4", "type": "concept", "title": "Trauma, testimony, and remembrance"}, {"id": "cf2f1db4-fbec-4c88-ace6-b19644592dd9", "type": "concept", "title": "History in public controversy"}], "description": "The unit connects history to collective identity and civic debate."}]}, {"id": "89322b0f-4140-4f17-b229-24f7dfea0dba", "type": "topic", "title": "Religion, Myth, Ritual, and Meaning-Making", "description": "Religious and mythic traditions are central to humanistic study because they shape art, literature, ethics, politics, time, community, and ideas of the sacred. This unit approaches religion academically, comparatively, and respectfully.", "children": [{"id": "d0dd2f88-8c9f-4fa2-8cad-6816d957023a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Academic study of religion", "children": [{"id": "a101653b-504b-40c7-92b7-fd328abb3683", "type": "concept", "title": "Myth, ritual, doctrine, ethics, and community"}, {"id": "57af8305-932a-4eae-a112-42ab7142e6c1", "type": "concept", "title": "Insider and outsider perspectives"}, {"id": "c29faaae-00e6-481f-9963-75aca690ad6c", "type": "concept", "title": "Text, practice, material culture, and lived religion"}, {"id": "5273b1c0-592f-4e61-8871-dec00597c24a", "type": "concept", "title": "Comparison and its limits"}], "description": "Students learn to analyze religion without reducing it to belief alone or evaluating it devotionally."}, {"id": "9e2678b1-0d6b-4942-8285-ead81124486b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Myth and symbolic worlds", "children": [{"id": "48c6dc2b-e801-4720-b6d7-e0d7d439d34e", "type": "concept", "title": "Creation and origin stories"}, {"id": "d4645c85-d63b-42de-a633-e4503c76cb24", "type": "concept", "title": "Heroes, tricksters, gods, and ancestors"}, {"id": "dc14150a-43a5-4822-bf09-c00aace38a2f", "type": "concept", "title": "Sacred time and sacred space"}, {"id": "c50728ed-5e1e-45c1-a419-729caf23f6fb", "type": "concept", "title": "Myth in modern culture"}], "description": "Students examine how stories organize cosmology, identity, social order, and human purpose."}, {"id": "a235a5dd-aebf-446c-9589-bab7e37ff194", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ritual, practice, and ethics", "children": [{"id": "df7e4d63-8acf-469d-b52a-673bc975c6bc", "type": "concept", "title": "Pilgrimage, prayer, festival, and sacrifice"}, {"id": "76ca07c5-c6d8-4f3e-be82-866611bd40e4", "type": "concept", "title": "Rites of passage"}, {"id": "b9f62e65-5f29-4bef-a151-3d4f98e84b03", "type": "concept", "title": "Law, ethics, and care"}, {"id": "10b18951-45c2-438f-94f0-764fb0e702ee", "type": "concept", "title": "Religion in public life"}], "description": "The goal is to understand religion as embodied, communal, and moral practice."}]}, {"id": "6e62dd86-69fb-4db5-8114-526d7442d8af", "type": "topic", "title": "Language, Translation, Rhetoric, and Communication", "description": "Language is the medium through which much of the humanities travels. This unit helps students see language as form, power, translation, persuasion, identity, and historical record.", "children": [{"id": "6b112742-9720-4f68-9d6e-cfbe951d81e0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Language and meaning", "children": [{"id": "339166f8-a9c5-40e9-927e-f8893244f550", "type": "concept", "title": "Denotation and connotation"}, {"id": "8cb61a97-2357-4eb3-8ff1-719ad66a2475", "type": "concept", "title": "Metaphor and figurative meaning"}, {"id": "2bec29f5-9229-4992-a3e2-7c34a8c00397", "type": "concept", "title": "Dialect, register, and code-switching"}, {"id": "493f0839-b7a1-480a-aa5f-832418561d0c", "type": "concept", "title": "Language change and etymology"}], "description": "Students learn that words carry histories, assumptions, connotations, and cultural worlds."}, {"id": "cd77406a-279d-4a0f-86be-230bfac2bbf6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Translation and world literature", "children": [{"id": "fbc37533-6cf0-42ec-a0f5-23409a086cfc", "type": "concept", "title": "Literal and interpretive translation"}, {"id": "252d842d-a015-4ea1-8e5f-944d72264dfb", "type": "concept", "title": "Untranslatable terms and cultural context"}, {"id": "fbca3bb6-1d0e-4e05-a15f-8719a9cbbc5f", "type": "concept", "title": "Adaptation and reception"}, {"id": "0a2b604a-d9c2-4224-bb62-dea562c7462d", "type": "concept", "title": "Translator visibility and ethics"}], "description": "Students examine what changes when works move across languages, media, audiences, and time."}, {"id": "7252a615-501e-4daa-960e-7aabc017a52c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "children": [{"id": "2ce15761-66e5-4dbc-ac55-087ebc1c3e64", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethos, pathos, and logos"}, {"id": "9a49fba7-e820-4041-8c0e-c917a6f69ac4", "type": "concept", "title": "Style, arrangement, and audience"}, {"id": "b86c0ba1-7148-482e-87bb-cca71b5dde2f", "type": "concept", "title": "Propaganda and public argument"}, {"id": "abe0e5b4-64fd-4864-9785-8b96fcadab9b", "type": "concept", "title": "Digital rhetoric and attention"}], "description": "The unit teaches students to analyze how language moves audiences and shapes public life."}]}, {"id": "d3eba38a-f9b2-4a7b-b0cb-a27c8b94572a", "type": "topic", "title": "Cross-Cultural Encounters, Global Traditions, and Comparative Humanities", "description": "A world-class humanities course must move beyond a single canon. This unit asks students to compare works across regions and traditions while avoiding flattening, exoticizing, or treating cultures as static.", "children": [{"id": "48e25cae-9b55-49e6-b2a3-11ad7a4e356a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Comparative method", "children": [{"id": "c21e7cd8-d194-404a-b0f1-cc2fa3cf4aca", "type": "concept", "title": "Common questions, different forms"}, {"id": "34ecf56d-05bb-4c76-a11d-4da174688747", "type": "concept", "title": "Translation and mediation"}, {"id": "5fafc296-802d-45b6-99e9-ccf1fa9c42f0", "type": "concept", "title": "Context before generalization"}, {"id": "e22b2884-1b8d-4664-8702-e3c353bdc4cd", "type": "concept", "title": "Limits of analogy"}], "description": "Students learn how to compare responsibly: with specificity, context, and awareness of unequal power."}, {"id": "7018bff1-e525-4838-82c7-1f40f4e36735", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Global traditions and circulation", "children": [{"id": "fe241dda-1e91-452b-ab86-b8a08aebff54", "type": "concept", "title": "Silk roads and oceanic exchange"}, {"id": "b564cd1f-acb3-45ae-9c23-028324fc7e93", "type": "concept", "title": "Colonialism and cultural encounter"}, {"id": "6a66f720-8267-4468-ad2d-fe8f36da4f46", "type": "concept", "title": "Diaspora and migration"}, {"id": "65cee746-3ef1-44f5-9888-ce7114a23575", "type": "concept", "title": "Global modernisms and contemporary culture"}], "description": "Students examine how ideas, texts, objects, and practices move through trade, empire, migration, pilgrimage, and media."}, {"id": "bddbd77a-de2a-4c88-9a46-e0b5b51b879b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Canon, inclusion, and curriculum", "children": [{"id": "2d3d39da-1ba4-4844-a49f-f4b59a123bdb", "type": "concept", "title": "Canon formation"}, {"id": "0f5207c1-5acc-4db9-bfe8-eb731e272811", "type": "concept", "title": "Marginalized voices and recovery work"}, {"id": "f13539ef-a62c-4f13-924e-de02bdef437a", "type": "concept", "title": "Indigenous and decolonial perspectives"}, {"id": "4225aa1f-265e-4e6c-96dc-d89da4cb3972", "type": "concept", "title": "World literature and world art debates"}], "description": "The unit asks who gets preserved, taught, translated, funded, and remembered."}]}, {"id": "332864df-1d89-4ca7-b31f-ca20ba12ff62", "type": "topic", "title": "Aesthetics, Beauty, Taste, and Judgment", "description": "Aesthetics helps students ask how humans experience, value, and debate art, beauty, sublimity, ugliness, pleasure, and form. The unit links feeling to argument rather than treating taste as merely personal.", "children": [{"id": "d223bc64-f0c2-4f5d-83a3-083c102f316e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Theories of beauty and art", "children": [{"id": "f4396248-5bd9-4f8c-a8ed-c20a168c7d1e", "type": "concept", "title": "Beauty, sublimity, and the picturesque"}, {"id": "469bdde6-fc5b-4e7f-8446-1853ff8c8351", "type": "concept", "title": "Mimesis, expression, form, and concept"}, {"id": "d116e118-4cdf-43b9-9bed-8669d1fe4f34", "type": "concept", "title": "Art, craft, and design"}, {"id": "cfb30f43-1a8a-44d2-a09a-ffcd9019d08d", "type": "concept", "title": "Institutional and social theories of art"}], "description": "Students encounter classic and contemporary debates about what makes something art and how aesthetic value is argued."}, {"id": "8b6d2ab1-0aa7-4b3e-b9fb-ca7f8890de05", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Taste, judgment, and criticism", "children": [{"id": "b6b407bc-063e-413f-95ac-ecac831d7c1a", "type": "concept", "title": "Criteria and evidence"}, {"id": "619e5014-fcc5-476d-986d-a7c62e45168e", "type": "concept", "title": "Reception and audience"}, {"id": "a208e25e-b623-4044-92cf-d566819dd02f", "type": "concept", "title": "Criticism as public writing"}, {"id": "83e6a207-db4b-4e59-910f-f14cc93db88c", "type": "concept", "title": "Disagreement and pluralism"}], "description": "Students practice making evaluative claims that are more rigorous than preference."}, {"id": "25def3eb-0300-4298-8b6d-35f92cafacab", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Aesthetic experience in everyday life", "children": [{"id": "88f51415-0210-4747-b213-d517e333c6c0", "type": "concept", "title": "Popular culture and taste hierarchies"}, {"id": "ef08943a-ff7d-4870-9c93-48fb6e233318", "type": "concept", "title": "Design and everyday objects"}, {"id": "4accdcee-cd78-4b59-976c-877efba7771a", "type": "concept", "title": "Environment and the sublime"}, {"id": "347d124c-e411-4ecd-b2dc-f3daaa644104", "type": "concept", "title": "Attention, boredom, and wonder"}], "description": "The unit expands aesthetics beyond museums to media, design, food, fashion, nature, and digital culture."}]}, {"id": "61c758f6-342e-4770-be4c-1a96154d23d3", "type": "topic", "title": "Modernity, Technology, Media, and the Digital Humanities", "description": "This unit connects the humanities to modern media systems and digital methods. Students consider how print, photography, film, the internet, data, and AI change cultural memory, authorship, interpretation, and access.", "children": [{"id": "5fd368f8-99bb-423b-9e6d-2546232ed34d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Media and modern life", "children": [{"id": "c68cb66c-8b20-4484-b0e4-de52caf2c01d", "type": "concept", "title": "Manuscript, print, photography, film, and digital media"}, {"id": "585a4273-b850-4aeb-a4fc-e23a15d8021f", "type": "concept", "title": "Mass culture and popular culture"}, {"id": "c8ba4876-06a4-451f-8a80-695d9d481623", "type": "concept", "title": "Platforms and attention"}, {"id": "f5902846-9663-405b-96fd-2585d3ea6807", "type": "concept", "title": "Authorship, remix, and participation"}], "description": "Students learn that humanities objects are shaped by technologies of production and circulation."}, {"id": "26720ba0-794a-4d63-8ca3-2879062ea687", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Digital humanities methods", "children": [{"id": "8d953adf-e757-4348-b766-5ef7adc33b15", "type": "concept", "title": "Text encoding and digital editions"}, {"id": "b63f78c6-b391-4569-906e-d88d26cae41a", "type": "concept", "title": "Mapping and timelines"}, {"id": "2c0564d0-b099-4910-a51a-ca5c6207f2dd", "type": "concept", "title": "Text mining and distant reading"}, {"id": "d18bdb61-3ee4-4d62-bd53-52121a3dbb18", "type": "concept", "title": "Digital archives and metadata"}], "description": "Students receive an introductory sense of how digital tools can support, but not replace, interpretation."}, {"id": "83302bc2-3f4b-406b-b744-41230d1432bb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "AI, interpretation, and human judgment", "children": [{"id": "e304b53a-f0ed-438b-8d2d-e042f57e0f4d", "type": "concept", "title": "Generative AI and authorship"}, {"id": "e78af9e7-3915-4fc4-b4ee-f86d984d77b9", "type": "concept", "title": "Algorithmic bias and cultural data"}, {"id": "7e19fcad-f4d6-4db2-aa68-4e9d99062cb0", "type": "concept", "title": "Citation and disclosure"}, {"id": "c9fc2566-0d6e-49b9-9b28-49520cad5a46", "type": "concept", "title": "What human interpretation still does"}], "description": "The goal is to examine new computational tools through humanistic questions about evidence, bias, creativity, and responsibility."}]}, {"id": "2190ecc6-34c7-4da2-ad88-e43d516da469", "type": "topic", "title": "Power, Identity, Justice, and Public Humanities", "description": "The humanities study how cultures represent people, justify power, imagine justice, and contest exclusion. This unit brings identity, inequality, public memory, and civic dialogue into the center of interpretation.", "children": [{"id": "1fda4141-0221-4075-a297-d348ca437742", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Representation and identity", "children": [{"id": "5b013883-58b0-485a-b033-059ab4f077ff", "type": "concept", "title": "Stereotype and counter-narrative"}, {"id": "9b87802c-7f8c-419e-b3f7-551a3b8c8b65", "type": "concept", "title": "Voice, agency, and authorship"}, {"id": "7e05c3b2-2e9b-46b6-a48e-a7a0b49f2d08", "type": "concept", "title": "Intersectionality"}, {"id": "a387c854-393b-4e2c-a967-762f49132796", "type": "concept", "title": "Embodiment and lived experience"}], "description": "Students analyze how race, gender, class, sexuality, disability, nation, and religion are represented and debated."}, {"id": "8230ea00-2b61-4014-bb29-1087663df95b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Power, institutions, and cultural authority", "children": [{"id": "a7e07d03-8afd-4584-ba75-6005d7d4c2d9", "type": "concept", "title": "Gatekeeping and access"}, {"id": "a9b201b6-c931-496b-b06d-f7a44efc9257", "type": "concept", "title": "Censorship and patronage"}, {"id": "f8ef813e-b458-44d0-b7bb-d6ccd5d89474", "type": "concept", "title": "Cultural property and ownership"}, {"id": "97374575-f553-4366-8012-dc44f90edbc3", "type": "concept", "title": "Public funding and cultural value"}], "description": "Students ask how schools, museums, publishers, archives, media, and states shape what becomes visible."}, {"id": "65488dee-a1d3-42af-8ff8-18a3d63bdf44", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Public humanities and civic dialogue", "children": [{"id": "8da7f868-fc0a-4592-ac48-13d1be31b88f", "type": "concept", "title": "Public-facing writing"}, {"id": "7203ecdd-1c1e-408a-8401-f19ddafd2760", "type": "concept", "title": "Community archives and oral history"}, {"id": "aec23bff-cae7-4085-ad5d-a6f8c3ce7650", "type": "concept", "title": "Dialogue across difference"}, {"id": "4f1b6057-c646-4bf8-9a70-b5912980d64a", "type": "concept", "title": "Humanities careers and civic practice"}], "description": "The unit connects classroom interpretation to community projects, public scholarship, museums, libraries, and democratic life."}]}, {"id": "89de399a-6267-4c57-8929-335b9706adca", "type": "topic", "title": "Humanities Research, Sources, Citation, and Ethical Scholarship", "description": "This unit teaches students how to research responsibly in the humanities: finding sources, evaluating authority, building bibliographies, citing correctly, and treating cultural materials with care.", "children": [{"id": "a86c977e-7acc-4630-ba7a-a260a077852e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Research design", "children": [{"id": "d2d43a50-8e55-4b54-be84-cb2d986455af", "type": "concept", "title": "Topic narrowing"}, {"id": "a8b8128c-5bae-4418-948c-de4806e63343", "type": "concept", "title": "Research questions"}, {"id": "f3efebc2-5ced-41a3-97d8-575fe258db14", "type": "concept", "title": "Working thesis"}, {"id": "3ba6b3dd-0742-4fc4-956d-c05295398920", "type": "concept", "title": "Annotated bibliography"}], "description": "Students learn to move from curiosity to a focused, answerable, interpretive question."}, {"id": "8db2367e-0caf-42b4-a5f1-dc8b0c4a47e7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sources and evidence", "children": [{"id": "05374e84-1fdb-4a52-8615-59b4ba4a0ee5", "type": "concept", "title": "Primary texts, objects, images, and recordings"}, {"id": "624b7bab-5bf8-4293-813a-82b7ce5a5fd5", "type": "concept", "title": "Scholarly books and articles"}, {"id": "6571463a-9a58-4421-9fec-1b8a12da9199", "type": "concept", "title": "Reference works and research guides"}, {"id": "9b6e8ebd-7121-4a37-93a4-e50bb12ace77", "type": "concept", "title": "Digital archives and databases"}], "description": "Students practice distinguishing primary, secondary, and reference sources and using each appropriately."}, {"id": "f729e97f-f41c-443d-bc14-a062f2c4990e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ethics, citation, and academic integrity", "children": [{"id": "a8994da4-7c00-4437-a18a-83787d2db28b", "type": "concept", "title": "Quotation, paraphrase, and summary"}, {"id": "3d9f097b-170b-449c-881b-07950498df08", "type": "concept", "title": "MLA and Chicago basics"}, {"id": "1edc1d7a-67bb-4797-9f6d-f5e2863025a7", "type": "concept", "title": "Plagiarism and patchwriting"}, {"id": "14293d0f-7842-4cad-a725-d93eaaafb0a5", "type": "concept", "title": "Cultural sensitivity and respectful use"}], "description": "The point is to understand citation as participation in a community of knowledge, not a formatting chore."}]}, {"id": "c3724dba-d20c-47db-a2a2-6da63eea5d22", "type": "topic", "title": "Capstone Humanistic Interpretation: Curate, Present, Reflect, and Connect to Modern Life", "description": "The capstone asks students to synthesize the course by interpreting a humanities object or question for an audience. They build a polished project that demonstrates evidence, context, argument, reflection, and public communication.", "children": [{"id": "ade715e6-893b-4798-8161-c73756047e38", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Project proposal and object selection", "children": [{"id": "ec458bdd-332c-4d30-947b-7ad6bc2083da", "type": "concept", "title": "Object or question rationale"}, {"id": "c31e55bb-ef4d-4e01-b062-5aaf55213b5c", "type": "concept", "title": "Research plan"}, {"id": "ac14fd71-27b2-4cca-ac61-a8ecf87941b7", "type": "concept", "title": "Audience and purpose"}, {"id": "8ead38ec-b385-46ff-a2d0-2205e5254f45", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethical considerations"}], "description": "Students choose a text, artwork, performance, place, artifact, idea, or cultural debate that rewards humanistic analysis."}, {"id": "b2c2796c-d432-4feb-b5c5-07b3f060cc15", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Interpretive project design", "children": [{"id": "3fbdd12e-438f-466b-bbfa-b41dc60c9d70", "type": "concept", "title": "Argument and structure"}, {"id": "f26ed83e-6ea8-4d70-86e9-bfeca2ce707f", "type": "concept", "title": "Multimodal evidence"}, {"id": "b0a980a8-a1dc-4786-b336-f1c130fa8bae", "type": "concept", "title": "Visual and textual clarity"}, {"id": "428f813d-fc09-40bb-86f3-c18b1b52ff44", "type": "concept", "title": "Accessibility and citation"}], "description": "Students translate research into a clear form such as an essay, exhibit, podcast, presentation, digital exhibit, or public-facing guide."}, {"id": "e0888d3b-4ac5-4948-bfee-f842134f4828", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reflection and transfer", "children": [{"id": "bfac4289-809f-4523-ab79-57e9491949c0", "type": "concept", "title": "Process reflection"}, {"id": "5cfb51c8-8ddc-4002-8eca-4ade36826cfe", "type": "concept", "title": "Peer critique and revision"}, {"id": "9757b5ed-ca32-4962-9d3b-cf6db9ecad30", "type": "concept", "title": "Connection to modern life"}, {"id": "d7ed17fb-49e6-4e28-9619-be8890d199da", "type": "concept", "title": "Portfolio-ready documentation"}], "description": "The final step asks students to articulate what humanistic inquiry changes in how they read, see, listen, judge, and participate in public life."}]}], "dbBacked": true}$ihumjson$::jsonb,
  true,
  false,
  'course',
  'public'
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.learning_paths where slug = 'introduction-to-the-humanities'
);

update public.learning_paths
set
  title = 'Introduction to the Humanities',
  goal = 'Introduction to the Humanities',
  summary = 'Introduction to the Humanities is a first-year course in the study of human meaning, expression, memory, value, and culture. Students read literature, examine philosophy, analyze visual art and architecture, listen to music, interpret performance, study history and religion, and investigate how language, media, power, and identity shape cultural life. The course emphasizes close reading, close looking, historical context, ethical reasoning, cross-cultural comparison, research literacy, and public communication.',
  data = $ihumjson${"slug": "introduction-to-the-humanities", "title": "Introduction to the Humanities", "description": "Introduction to the Humanities is a first-year course in the study of human meaning, expression, memory, value, and culture. Students read literature, examine philosophy, analyze visual art and architecture, listen to music, interpret performance, study history and religion, and investigate how language, media, power, and identity shape cultural life. The course emphasizes close reading, close looking, historical context, ethical reasoning, cross-cultural comparison, research literacy, and public communication.", "topics": [{"id": "caa984b6-fa9b-45d4-852d-5e2eeb5221b3", "type": "topic", "title": "What Are the Humanities? Questions, Methods, and Human Meaning", "description": "This opening unit defines the humanities as disciplines that study meaning, value, expression, memory, and human experience. Students learn why literature, philosophy, art, history, religion, music, performance, and language remain essential for interpreting culture and modern life.", "children": [{"id": "2946eb4f-27d6-4347-bdfa-f245b7e528c6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "The scope of humanistic study", "children": [{"id": "9645575b-ab00-4ce8-949a-d10e011bbfa1", "type": "concept", "title": "Meaning, value, interpretation, and form"}, {"id": "59bd6aa9-0d39-49b5-bf9e-7297ae6e2052", "type": "concept", "title": "Texts, artifacts, performances, images, and places"}, {"id": "0ac45513-10f3-4edb-9de2-f2c60a7c9715", "type": "concept", "title": "Humanistic questions about identity, justice, mortality, beauty, and belonging"}, {"id": "6d82bdea-3f04-494e-acba-9d03ff944a8b", "type": "concept", "title": "Connections to liberal arts education"}], "description": "Students learn what distinguishes humanities inquiry from scientific and social-scientific explanation while also seeing where the fields overlap."}, {"id": "1c6a7ed1-ab99-47d0-aabf-433461b5ff4c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Humanities as methods of attention", "children": [{"id": "c8598667-50fc-4701-bf9f-15ba4cdde364", "type": "concept", "title": "Slow reading and close looking"}, {"id": "437c2c62-76e7-435a-8a69-fed610cf845c", "type": "concept", "title": "Description before interpretation"}, {"id": "9199d2f9-4d3d-4a4e-a677-6ce2e47f6d3c", "type": "concept", "title": "Historical and cultural framing"}, {"id": "33ce7cea-7237-44d9-9bac-4ad12ba8b0f7", "type": "concept", "title": "Ambiguity, evidence, and multiple meanings"}], "description": "The point is to cultivate careful noticing before judgment: reading, listening, looking, comparing, and contextualizing."}, {"id": "bfc70754-f2b9-485c-ba06-1b78ea5e4397", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Why the humanities matter now", "children": [{"id": "f356dcfb-34a4-428c-8aa1-2e9607fd8b7d", "type": "concept", "title": "Citizenship and civic imagination"}, {"id": "50aca8ef-3a8f-442f-9f69-23978f121e97", "type": "concept", "title": "Empathy and perspective-taking"}, {"id": "4d3f3272-4fb4-4368-a073-5053c4a676f1", "type": "concept", "title": "Cultural memory and public debate"}, {"id": "42f21ee4-d633-4aae-adbf-f994d8920d86", "type": "concept", "title": "Humanistic skills in contemporary careers"}], "description": "Students connect the humanities to public life, democratic dialogue, ethical judgment, creativity, and self-understanding."}]}, {"id": "34c03ea5-1517-48cf-bd1e-3428030acd83", "type": "topic", "title": "Humanistic Inquiry: Close Reading, Interpretation, Context, and Argument", "description": "This unit gives students the toolkit for doing humanities work. They practice turning observation into interpretation, interpretation into argument, and argument into clear academic writing.", "children": [{"id": "26546832-b1c8-4981-9875-4e9e67ba3d85", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Close reading and close looking", "children": [{"id": "b5b2ab73-79a4-4c76-925c-e3a744977b20", "type": "concept", "title": "Diction, image, metaphor, structure, and genre"}, {"id": "8be17831-8017-454c-8224-eb657bcd8eb8", "type": "concept", "title": "Composition, line, color, material, and scale"}, {"id": "2f804f09-32cf-45e6-810b-dc450e38f005", "type": "concept", "title": "Sound, rhythm, gesture, and performance detail"}, {"id": "d9877ae5-8ae2-4f5d-b85b-81c27e8a38ca", "type": "concept", "title": "Patterns, tensions, and anomalies"}], "description": "Students learn to build interpretations from specific evidence rather than broad impressions."}, {"id": "975177f2-a165-450b-8d50-f4b16cd050b2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Context and historical method", "children": [{"id": "57f585e6-88ce-4fed-9fcb-87401691ad0f", "type": "concept", "title": "Author, maker, patron, and audience"}, {"id": "56a9604b-df12-466a-a4e3-ed858f5e0402", "type": "concept", "title": "Period, place, movement, and medium"}, {"id": "73b21331-9f7f-4150-b2f9-00bb9f4acbe2", "type": "concept", "title": "Reception and changing interpretation"}, {"id": "b1b9ae3e-0291-4448-abb4-34843eab9636", "type": "concept", "title": "Archives and primary sources"}], "description": "Students learn that works are made inside worlds: institutions, languages, audiences, technologies, and power relations."}, {"id": "ad3baee6-ef01-44e6-9c87-26721a43d08c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Argument and scholarly conversation", "children": [{"id": "02396f8a-074d-4e47-8fde-31d51ef72dee", "type": "concept", "title": "Research questions and thesis statements"}, {"id": "2e0bde40-b1fa-47c9-80f9-ef6fbc01db7e", "type": "concept", "title": "Evidence selection and explanation"}, {"id": "187014fc-b7f0-4258-ad4f-1d7cb26d5381", "type": "concept", "title": "Counterargument and revision"}, {"id": "38c150d8-e19c-4dbc-b761-faf45cda182f", "type": "concept", "title": "Citation and academic integrity"}], "description": "The gist is learning to enter a debate responsibly, with claims, evidence, warrants, and respectful disagreement."}]}, {"id": "21b5e6e6-5fdb-4b9e-9bf3-2f3507a1cd51", "type": "topic", "title": "Literature as World-Making: Poetry, Narrative, Drama, and Myth", "description": "Literature gives students practice interpreting language as art, memory, thought, and social imagination. The unit treats literary works as crafted forms that build worlds, voices, conflicts, and ethical possibilities.", "children": [{"id": "08ca7da2-eafb-453c-9483-6585747b88bb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Poetry and compressed language", "children": [{"id": "294fc7bf-fbef-4111-827c-35c492bfc6c4", "type": "concept", "title": "Speaker, tone, and address"}, {"id": "b4a47046-d42b-4da0-9cd7-22568efdc048", "type": "concept", "title": "Figurative language and imagery"}, {"id": "a27a7b2f-671c-4e71-b4c9-d879b45313c7", "type": "concept", "title": "Form, meter, line, and stanza"}, {"id": "43d28f2a-4ac5-4f95-8353-ecf66af51039", "type": "concept", "title": "Translation and performance"}], "description": "Students learn how poems make meaning through rhythm, image, sound, form, and silence."}, {"id": "7c368f92-49c5-4579-a2af-95ca954477df", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Narrative and fiction", "children": [{"id": "1aa7c6f4-babd-4a02-985c-41cea44fe648", "type": "concept", "title": "Plot, scene, and structure"}, {"id": "6839bc09-45c0-4e2a-85b0-69473b03f895", "type": "concept", "title": "Narrator and point of view"}, {"id": "c674ceef-4f98-42ce-8d4c-a17252e188e9", "type": "concept", "title": "Character, desire, and conflict"}, {"id": "293af96e-e70e-47e8-b3bc-0ba2e9a4c325", "type": "concept", "title": "World-building and social context"}], "description": "Students examine how stories organize time, causality, character, perspective, and moral complexity."}, {"id": "800f5096-b836-4d71-90a7-ddc9c1d4b4a5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Drama, myth, and collective imagination", "children": [{"id": "267631e5-6636-43c8-b37a-b1ce044bd062", "type": "concept", "title": "Tragedy, comedy, and ritual"}, {"id": "8f1c4d16-b897-4b36-b51f-63ef8a7695d8", "type": "concept", "title": "Mythic patterns and archetypes"}, {"id": "f548ff5a-a29b-4dbf-8d60-763a95c5e1a7", "type": "concept", "title": "Performance, audience, and embodiment"}, {"id": "b81a2ef6-60b4-4abd-8349-bc21e6b86fbd", "type": "concept", "title": "Adaptation and reinterpretation"}], "description": "Students explore texts meant for performance and stories that communities use to explain origins, values, and crisis."}]}, {"id": "22fa60a8-4963-419d-bdb3-bc568c0132f8", "type": "topic", "title": "Philosophy and the History of Ideas: Reality, Knowledge, Ethics, and the Self", "description": "Philosophy teaches students to ask precise questions, test assumptions, and evaluate reasons. This unit introduces major philosophical problems that shape humanistic inquiry across periods and cultures.", "children": [{"id": "67a656ea-5d41-46fe-bd0b-6476abf48a05", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reality, knowledge, and skepticism", "children": [{"id": "5b1bef6c-3469-4f59-9851-0d637fff3e9d", "type": "concept", "title": "Metaphysics and being"}, {"id": "fc072107-efec-440e-b5d7-e6649d693f74", "type": "concept", "title": "Epistemology and evidence"}, {"id": "ad432ec5-9223-4412-abb4-5409fc7f770f", "type": "concept", "title": "Skepticism and certainty"}, {"id": "2e934095-de01-42b0-8202-1f71dad74cc7", "type": "concept", "title": "Mind, body, and self"}], "description": "Students learn how philosophers question what exists, what can be known, and how belief can be justified."}, {"id": "9a3560ab-c6a4-470b-b7a1-4b8db7bfc1c8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ethics and moral reasoning", "children": [{"id": "097d414b-5968-47e0-a72a-3eacc79d396a", "type": "concept", "title": "Virtue ethics"}, {"id": "1587b7af-1375-43e3-b7b7-47aec1c18745", "type": "concept", "title": "Deontology and duty"}, {"id": "e9090a61-2a46-44d2-9553-28686fbef709", "type": "concept", "title": "Consequentialism"}, {"id": "af66f385-490d-4a9a-aa0a-4a3259fec359", "type": "concept", "title": "Care, responsibility, and applied ethics"}], "description": "Students practice comparing frameworks for judgment and applying them to difficult human situations."}, {"id": "05654193-24e9-43c0-9fde-66bbb0a95e07", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Political philosophy and justice", "children": [{"id": "14ee292a-4f47-4e61-aa90-69081f69ad64", "type": "concept", "title": "Liberty, equality, and rights"}, {"id": "9efda216-830c-4a59-8b64-0566e9d99d6d", "type": "concept", "title": "Authority and legitimacy"}, {"id": "8eea75f8-a34d-4341-9358-11a17171cf48", "type": "concept", "title": "Justice and distribution"}, {"id": "f2a5bdec-ecc7-4a98-a215-e80dec6a48e1", "type": "concept", "title": "Democracy, citizenship, and dissent"}], "description": "The unit connects ideas about the good life to institutions, rights, authority, and public life."}]}, {"id": "e4d88fd6-a22e-4137-8b3d-9d3061f21426", "type": "topic", "title": "Visual Arts and Architecture: Seeing, Form, Space, and Power", "description": "This unit trains visual literacy. Students learn to interpret images, objects, buildings, and spaces as cultural arguments shaped by materials, techniques, patrons, audiences, and histories.", "children": [{"id": "8965745d-b48a-4c01-bd3c-b2af2995ffae", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Formal visual analysis", "children": [{"id": "dde9130c-9a38-4ff8-bdf5-01e0ccc0411e", "type": "concept", "title": "Line, color, light, texture, and composition"}, {"id": "ad2356a5-16a1-411b-859c-10321d89717d", "type": "concept", "title": "Scale, proportion, balance, and movement"}, {"id": "7d7955ef-83df-4f9d-b893-b671c66d1295", "type": "concept", "title": "Medium, material, and technique"}, {"id": "4b9e01ea-3d33-4cff-91bf-9370e357a56f", "type": "concept", "title": "Iconography and symbolism"}], "description": "Students learn to describe what they see with precision before moving into meaning."}, {"id": "0b3d44e7-c406-4b5a-87a6-80d42e76388c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Architecture and built environments", "children": [{"id": "7696e6e0-7714-4c6a-8232-560f7565350a", "type": "concept", "title": "Function and form"}, {"id": "e9f570ad-718b-4533-9179-abf97468ef04", "type": "concept", "title": "Sacred, civic, domestic, and commercial spaces"}, {"id": "7d613167-6ff9-4276-a682-e638208a2e27", "type": "concept", "title": "Urbanism and landscape"}, {"id": "db3b9c0c-4842-4649-932d-3a825db5841e", "type": "concept", "title": "Monuments, memorials, and contested space"}], "description": "Students examine how space organizes social life, ritual, memory, authority, and belonging."}, {"id": "b96ffe8d-2872-4158-83ea-7bda3fb86a68", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Museums, display, and cultural power", "children": [{"id": "657d291e-c3e0-4c2b-a13e-992ed4428963", "type": "concept", "title": "Collection and provenance"}, {"id": "39f85171-2e18-4e5c-91e1-bb9746725798", "type": "concept", "title": "Curatorial framing"}, {"id": "239a5d28-8399-4110-8253-1c51210cd40e", "type": "concept", "title": "Repatriation and cultural property"}, {"id": "7a9038ed-ee07-48e6-ba8f-ff7b72ddbc94", "type": "concept", "title": "Public access and interpretation"}], "description": "The unit asks how art becomes heritage, who interprets it, and what responsibilities follow."}]}, {"id": "799fb55c-279d-4087-81f9-21d04583e381", "type": "topic", "title": "Music, Theater, Performance, and Embodied Culture", "description": "Humanities study is not only textual or visual. This unit examines live, embodied, and sonic forms that create meaning through voice, movement, time, audience, and ritual.", "children": [{"id": "30eb67e6-bffa-4598-857a-a7c63e7e2685", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Music and listening", "children": [{"id": "e4239f69-e8e5-45d6-bee1-03c8ea93e0f0", "type": "concept", "title": "Melody, rhythm, harmony, timbre, and texture"}, {"id": "bccc3868-d209-4bd1-b996-cc96096d24e4", "type": "concept", "title": "Genre and performance context"}, {"id": "3325ba59-c126-4fac-8b85-06c99bae7e12", "type": "concept", "title": "Sacred, courtly, popular, and experimental music"}, {"id": "01623dbd-4391-4afa-9a34-49e7d714cf90", "type": "concept", "title": "Listening journals and sonic evidence"}], "description": "Students learn to discuss music as patterned sound with cultural, emotional, and historical force."}, {"id": "df4e3748-580b-4ae9-b689-16e000882901", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Theater and performance", "children": [{"id": "a9b13adf-f0f8-46f3-9b18-d9c69ef58dab", "type": "concept", "title": "Script, staging, and dramaturgy"}, {"id": "4f711112-39a2-4cb0-a5e5-e1a5a50704bf", "type": "concept", "title": "Gesture, voice, costume, and set"}, {"id": "5f4e7546-fa96-48d0-8436-1905aabe8925", "type": "concept", "title": "Audience and liveness"}, {"id": "7a742e9f-014a-497b-928c-32b63f8f01f0", "type": "concept", "title": "Performance traditions across cultures"}], "description": "Students study performance as an event made by bodies, spaces, texts, audiences, and conventions."}, {"id": "f8e6a5a6-730c-48b5-a57e-b649a5c1a59a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Embodiment and cultural practice", "children": [{"id": "be8fa72e-a616-4914-96f7-9a8bb5ed93d7", "type": "concept", "title": "Ritual and ceremony"}, {"id": "10e58992-7876-4bfa-b903-b9df5a874b82", "type": "concept", "title": "Dance and movement"}, {"id": "a8c0b07f-131c-4f02-976e-3d55b607967c", "type": "concept", "title": "Everyday performance and identity"}, {"id": "5e376d9b-37d4-421f-bc35-e36ab9106ee9", "type": "concept", "title": "Documentation and performance archives"}], "description": "The goal is to recognize that culture is enacted, repeated, improvised, and contested through bodies."}]}, {"id": "63ae2170-eb7c-4eb3-92ff-221966c089e1", "type": "topic", "title": "History, Memory, Archives, and Cultural Context", "description": "History gives humanities students a disciplined way to study change over time. This unit distinguishes memory from evidence, narrative from archive, and interpretation from mere chronology.", "children": [{"id": "632da7e2-74b2-43ab-9661-73c00ada3369", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Historical thinking", "children": [{"id": "0028af8f-dd0b-4030-af15-1d63d168add5", "type": "concept", "title": "Chronology and periodization"}, {"id": "2248a9c3-bdd5-4f7e-9f54-459d22d4b8ff", "type": "concept", "title": "Cause, consequence, and contingency"}, {"id": "6ad5aaee-2073-4161-a314-2a1113db0bbd", "type": "concept", "title": "Comparison across places and periods"}, {"id": "b2507c7d-66c0-48ad-8e5b-a38b7cd567f0", "type": "concept", "title": "Microhistory and global history"}], "description": "Students learn to analyze causation, contingency, continuity, change, and periodization."}, {"id": "da7efa1c-cd81-45ed-a2fe-3342ba4c1691", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Archives and primary sources", "children": [{"id": "4a4bc01e-f1cd-494e-9e43-76410928df1e", "type": "concept", "title": "Manuscripts, letters, images, maps, and objects"}, {"id": "1d881805-96d1-4b2f-8d1c-28621c0201eb", "type": "concept", "title": "Source criticism and bias"}, {"id": "fadb71c5-b517-4972-bc6d-f1beb81a35ce", "type": "concept", "title": "Silences in the archive"}, {"id": "14223d27-4984-439a-9727-698f0e9ab462", "type": "concept", "title": "Oral history and lived memory"}], "description": "Students practice reading historical evidence as partial, situated, and often contested."}, {"id": "aee17fe5-bc3c-40f0-ac5e-0eb7b1db1a5a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Memory, commemoration, and public history", "children": [{"id": "410db3ff-8ab6-4ede-90b6-88db2bedb539", "type": "concept", "title": "Monuments and memorials"}, {"id": "e4091460-dee6-48b3-b4cf-4a9ac94d4595", "type": "concept", "title": "Museums and heritage sites"}, {"id": "8df803b5-8ced-48f3-b46d-87153f3a7be4", "type": "concept", "title": "Trauma, testimony, and remembrance"}, {"id": "cf2f1db4-fbec-4c88-ace6-b19644592dd9", "type": "concept", "title": "History in public controversy"}], "description": "The unit connects history to collective identity and civic debate."}]}, {"id": "89322b0f-4140-4f17-b229-24f7dfea0dba", "type": "topic", "title": "Religion, Myth, Ritual, and Meaning-Making", "description": "Religious and mythic traditions are central to humanistic study because they shape art, literature, ethics, politics, time, community, and ideas of the sacred. This unit approaches religion academically, comparatively, and respectfully.", "children": [{"id": "d0dd2f88-8c9f-4fa2-8cad-6816d957023a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Academic study of religion", "children": [{"id": "a101653b-504b-40c7-92b7-fd328abb3683", "type": "concept", "title": "Myth, ritual, doctrine, ethics, and community"}, {"id": "57af8305-932a-4eae-a112-42ab7142e6c1", "type": "concept", "title": "Insider and outsider perspectives"}, {"id": "c29faaae-00e6-481f-9963-75aca690ad6c", "type": "concept", "title": "Text, practice, material culture, and lived religion"}, {"id": "5273b1c0-592f-4e61-8871-dec00597c24a", "type": "concept", "title": "Comparison and its limits"}], "description": "Students learn to analyze religion without reducing it to belief alone or evaluating it devotionally."}, {"id": "9e2678b1-0d6b-4942-8285-ead81124486b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Myth and symbolic worlds", "children": [{"id": "48c6dc2b-e801-4720-b6d7-e0d7d439d34e", "type": "concept", "title": "Creation and origin stories"}, {"id": "d4645c85-d63b-42de-a633-e4503c76cb24", "type": "concept", "title": "Heroes, tricksters, gods, and ancestors"}, {"id": "dc14150a-43a5-4822-bf09-c00aace38a2f", "type": "concept", "title": "Sacred time and sacred space"}, {"id": "c50728ed-5e1e-45c1-a419-729caf23f6fb", "type": "concept", "title": "Myth in modern culture"}], "description": "Students examine how stories organize cosmology, identity, social order, and human purpose."}, {"id": "a235a5dd-aebf-446c-9589-bab7e37ff194", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ritual, practice, and ethics", "children": [{"id": "df7e4d63-8acf-469d-b52a-673bc975c6bc", "type": "concept", "title": "Pilgrimage, prayer, festival, and sacrifice"}, {"id": "76ca07c5-c6d8-4f3e-be82-866611bd40e4", "type": "concept", "title": "Rites of passage"}, {"id": "b9f62e65-5f29-4bef-a151-3d4f98e84b03", "type": "concept", "title": "Law, ethics, and care"}, {"id": "10b18951-45c2-438f-94f0-764fb0e702ee", "type": "concept", "title": "Religion in public life"}], "description": "The goal is to understand religion as embodied, communal, and moral practice."}]}, {"id": "6e62dd86-69fb-4db5-8114-526d7442d8af", "type": "topic", "title": "Language, Translation, Rhetoric, and Communication", "description": "Language is the medium through which much of the humanities travels. This unit helps students see language as form, power, translation, persuasion, identity, and historical record.", "children": [{"id": "6b112742-9720-4f68-9d6e-cfbe951d81e0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Language and meaning", "children": [{"id": "339166f8-a9c5-40e9-927e-f8893244f550", "type": "concept", "title": "Denotation and connotation"}, {"id": "8cb61a97-2357-4eb3-8ff1-719ad66a2475", "type": "concept", "title": "Metaphor and figurative meaning"}, {"id": "2bec29f5-9229-4992-a3e2-7c34a8c00397", "type": "concept", "title": "Dialect, register, and code-switching"}, {"id": "493f0839-b7a1-480a-aa5f-832418561d0c", "type": "concept", "title": "Language change and etymology"}], "description": "Students learn that words carry histories, assumptions, connotations, and cultural worlds."}, {"id": "cd77406a-279d-4a0f-86be-230bfac2bbf6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Translation and world literature", "children": [{"id": "fbc37533-6cf0-42ec-a0f5-23409a086cfc", "type": "concept", "title": "Literal and interpretive translation"}, {"id": "252d842d-a015-4ea1-8e5f-944d72264dfb", "type": "concept", "title": "Untranslatable terms and cultural context"}, {"id": "fbca3bb6-1d0e-4e05-a15f-8719a9cbbc5f", "type": "concept", "title": "Adaptation and reception"}, {"id": "0a2b604a-d9c2-4224-bb62-dea562c7462d", "type": "concept", "title": "Translator visibility and ethics"}], "description": "Students examine what changes when works move across languages, media, audiences, and time."}, {"id": "7252a615-501e-4daa-960e-7aabc017a52c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "children": [{"id": "2ce15761-66e5-4dbc-ac55-087ebc1c3e64", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethos, pathos, and logos"}, {"id": "9a49fba7-e820-4041-8c0e-c917a6f69ac4", "type": "concept", "title": "Style, arrangement, and audience"}, {"id": "b86c0ba1-7148-482e-87bb-cca71b5dde2f", "type": "concept", "title": "Propaganda and public argument"}, {"id": "abe0e5b4-64fd-4864-9785-8b96fcadab9b", "type": "concept", "title": "Digital rhetoric and attention"}], "description": "The unit teaches students to analyze how language moves audiences and shapes public life."}]}, {"id": "d3eba38a-f9b2-4a7b-b0cb-a27c8b94572a", "type": "topic", "title": "Cross-Cultural Encounters, Global Traditions, and Comparative Humanities", "description": "A world-class humanities course must move beyond a single canon. This unit asks students to compare works across regions and traditions while avoiding flattening, exoticizing, or treating cultures as static.", "children": [{"id": "48e25cae-9b55-49e6-b2a3-11ad7a4e356a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Comparative method", "children": [{"id": "c21e7cd8-d194-404a-b0f1-cc2fa3cf4aca", "type": "concept", "title": "Common questions, different forms"}, {"id": "34ecf56d-05bb-4c76-a11d-4da174688747", "type": "concept", "title": "Translation and mediation"}, {"id": "5fafc296-802d-45b6-99e9-ccf1fa9c42f0", "type": "concept", "title": "Context before generalization"}, {"id": "e22b2884-1b8d-4664-8702-e3c353bdc4cd", "type": "concept", "title": "Limits of analogy"}], "description": "Students learn how to compare responsibly: with specificity, context, and awareness of unequal power."}, {"id": "7018bff1-e525-4838-82c7-1f40f4e36735", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Global traditions and circulation", "children": [{"id": "fe241dda-1e91-452b-ab86-b8a08aebff54", "type": "concept", "title": "Silk roads and oceanic exchange"}, {"id": "b564cd1f-acb3-45ae-9c23-028324fc7e93", "type": "concept", "title": "Colonialism and cultural encounter"}, {"id": "6a66f720-8267-4468-ad2d-fe8f36da4f46", "type": "concept", "title": "Diaspora and migration"}, {"id": "65cee746-3ef1-44f5-9888-ce7114a23575", "type": "concept", "title": "Global modernisms and contemporary culture"}], "description": "Students examine how ideas, texts, objects, and practices move through trade, empire, migration, pilgrimage, and media."}, {"id": "bddbd77a-de2a-4c88-9a46-e0b5b51b879b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Canon, inclusion, and curriculum", "children": [{"id": "2d3d39da-1ba4-4844-a49f-f4b59a123bdb", "type": "concept", "title": "Canon formation"}, {"id": "0f5207c1-5acc-4db9-bfe8-eb731e272811", "type": "concept", "title": "Marginalized voices and recovery work"}, {"id": "f13539ef-a62c-4f13-924e-de02bdef437a", "type": "concept", "title": "Indigenous and decolonial perspectives"}, {"id": "4225aa1f-265e-4e6c-96dc-d89da4cb3972", "type": "concept", "title": "World literature and world art debates"}], "description": "The unit asks who gets preserved, taught, translated, funded, and remembered."}]}, {"id": "332864df-1d89-4ca7-b31f-ca20ba12ff62", "type": "topic", "title": "Aesthetics, Beauty, Taste, and Judgment", "description": "Aesthetics helps students ask how humans experience, value, and debate art, beauty, sublimity, ugliness, pleasure, and form. The unit links feeling to argument rather than treating taste as merely personal.", "children": [{"id": "d223bc64-f0c2-4f5d-83a3-083c102f316e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Theories of beauty and art", "children": [{"id": "f4396248-5bd9-4f8c-a8ed-c20a168c7d1e", "type": "concept", "title": "Beauty, sublimity, and the picturesque"}, {"id": "469bdde6-fc5b-4e7f-8446-1853ff8c8351", "type": "concept", "title": "Mimesis, expression, form, and concept"}, {"id": "d116e118-4cdf-43b9-9bed-8669d1fe4f34", "type": "concept", "title": "Art, craft, and design"}, {"id": "cfb30f43-1a8a-44d2-a09a-ffcd9019d08d", "type": "concept", "title": "Institutional and social theories of art"}], "description": "Students encounter classic and contemporary debates about what makes something art and how aesthetic value is argued."}, {"id": "8b6d2ab1-0aa7-4b3e-b9fb-ca7f8890de05", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Taste, judgment, and criticism", "children": [{"id": "b6b407bc-063e-413f-95ac-ecac831d7c1a", "type": "concept", "title": "Criteria and evidence"}, {"id": "619e5014-fcc5-476d-986d-a7c62e45168e", "type": "concept", "title": "Reception and audience"}, {"id": "a208e25e-b623-4044-92cf-d566819dd02f", "type": "concept", "title": "Criticism as public writing"}, {"id": "83e6a207-db4b-4e59-910f-f14cc93db88c", "type": "concept", "title": "Disagreement and pluralism"}], "description": "Students practice making evaluative claims that are more rigorous than preference."}, {"id": "25def3eb-0300-4298-8b6d-35f92cafacab", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Aesthetic experience in everyday life", "children": [{"id": "88f51415-0210-4747-b213-d517e333c6c0", "type": "concept", "title": "Popular culture and taste hierarchies"}, {"id": "ef08943a-ff7d-4870-9c93-48fb6e233318", "type": "concept", "title": "Design and everyday objects"}, {"id": "4accdcee-cd78-4b59-976c-877efba7771a", "type": "concept", "title": "Environment and the sublime"}, {"id": "347d124c-e411-4ecd-b2dc-f3daaa644104", "type": "concept", "title": "Attention, boredom, and wonder"}], "description": "The unit expands aesthetics beyond museums to media, design, food, fashion, nature, and digital culture."}]}, {"id": "61c758f6-342e-4770-be4c-1a96154d23d3", "type": "topic", "title": "Modernity, Technology, Media, and the Digital Humanities", "description": "This unit connects the humanities to modern media systems and digital methods. Students consider how print, photography, film, the internet, data, and AI change cultural memory, authorship, interpretation, and access.", "children": [{"id": "5fd368f8-99bb-423b-9e6d-2546232ed34d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Media and modern life", "children": [{"id": "c68cb66c-8b20-4484-b0e4-de52caf2c01d", "type": "concept", "title": "Manuscript, print, photography, film, and digital media"}, {"id": "585a4273-b850-4aeb-a4fc-e23a15d8021f", "type": "concept", "title": "Mass culture and popular culture"}, {"id": "c8ba4876-06a4-451f-8a80-695d9d481623", "type": "concept", "title": "Platforms and attention"}, {"id": "f5902846-9663-405b-96fd-2585d3ea6807", "type": "concept", "title": "Authorship, remix, and participation"}], "description": "Students learn that humanities objects are shaped by technologies of production and circulation."}, {"id": "26720ba0-794a-4d63-8ca3-2879062ea687", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Digital humanities methods", "children": [{"id": "8d953adf-e757-4348-b766-5ef7adc33b15", "type": "concept", "title": "Text encoding and digital editions"}, {"id": "b63f78c6-b391-4569-906e-d88d26cae41a", "type": "concept", "title": "Mapping and timelines"}, {"id": "2c0564d0-b099-4910-a51a-ca5c6207f2dd", "type": "concept", "title": "Text mining and distant reading"}, {"id": "d18bdb61-3ee4-4d62-bd53-52121a3dbb18", "type": "concept", "title": "Digital archives and metadata"}], "description": "Students receive an introductory sense of how digital tools can support, but not replace, interpretation."}, {"id": "83302bc2-3f4b-406b-b744-41230d1432bb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "AI, interpretation, and human judgment", "children": [{"id": "e304b53a-f0ed-438b-8d2d-e042f57e0f4d", "type": "concept", "title": "Generative AI and authorship"}, {"id": "e78af9e7-3915-4fc4-b4ee-f86d984d77b9", "type": "concept", "title": "Algorithmic bias and cultural data"}, {"id": "7e19fcad-f4d6-4db2-aa68-4e9d99062cb0", "type": "concept", "title": "Citation and disclosure"}, {"id": "c9fc2566-0d6e-49b9-9b28-49520cad5a46", "type": "concept", "title": "What human interpretation still does"}], "description": "The goal is to examine new computational tools through humanistic questions about evidence, bias, creativity, and responsibility."}]}, {"id": "2190ecc6-34c7-4da2-ad88-e43d516da469", "type": "topic", "title": "Power, Identity, Justice, and Public Humanities", "description": "The humanities study how cultures represent people, justify power, imagine justice, and contest exclusion. This unit brings identity, inequality, public memory, and civic dialogue into the center of interpretation.", "children": [{"id": "1fda4141-0221-4075-a297-d348ca437742", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Representation and identity", "children": [{"id": "5b013883-58b0-485a-b033-059ab4f077ff", "type": "concept", "title": "Stereotype and counter-narrative"}, {"id": "9b87802c-7f8c-419e-b3f7-551a3b8c8b65", "type": "concept", "title": "Voice, agency, and authorship"}, {"id": "7e05c3b2-2e9b-46b6-a48e-a7a0b49f2d08", "type": "concept", "title": "Intersectionality"}, {"id": "a387c854-393b-4e2c-a967-762f49132796", "type": "concept", "title": "Embodiment and lived experience"}], "description": "Students analyze how race, gender, class, sexuality, disability, nation, and religion are represented and debated."}, {"id": "8230ea00-2b61-4014-bb29-1087663df95b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Power, institutions, and cultural authority", "children": [{"id": "a7e07d03-8afd-4584-ba75-6005d7d4c2d9", "type": "concept", "title": "Gatekeeping and access"}, {"id": "a9b201b6-c931-496b-b06d-f7a44efc9257", "type": "concept", "title": "Censorship and patronage"}, {"id": "f8ef813e-b458-44d0-b7bb-d6ccd5d89474", "type": "concept", "title": "Cultural property and ownership"}, {"id": "97374575-f553-4366-8012-dc44f90edbc3", "type": "concept", "title": "Public funding and cultural value"}], "description": "Students ask how schools, museums, publishers, archives, media, and states shape what becomes visible."}, {"id": "65488dee-a1d3-42af-8ff8-18a3d63bdf44", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Public humanities and civic dialogue", "children": [{"id": "8da7f868-fc0a-4592-ac48-13d1be31b88f", "type": "concept", "title": "Public-facing writing"}, {"id": "7203ecdd-1c1e-408a-8401-f19ddafd2760", "type": "concept", "title": "Community archives and oral history"}, {"id": "aec23bff-cae7-4085-ad5d-a6f8c3ce7650", "type": "concept", "title": "Dialogue across difference"}, {"id": "4f1b6057-c646-4bf8-9a70-b5912980d64a", "type": "concept", "title": "Humanities careers and civic practice"}], "description": "The unit connects classroom interpretation to community projects, public scholarship, museums, libraries, and democratic life."}]}, {"id": "89de399a-6267-4c57-8929-335b9706adca", "type": "topic", "title": "Humanities Research, Sources, Citation, and Ethical Scholarship", "description": "This unit teaches students how to research responsibly in the humanities: finding sources, evaluating authority, building bibliographies, citing correctly, and treating cultural materials with care.", "children": [{"id": "a86c977e-7acc-4630-ba7a-a260a077852e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Research design", "children": [{"id": "d2d43a50-8e55-4b54-be84-cb2d986455af", "type": "concept", "title": "Topic narrowing"}, {"id": "a8b8128c-5bae-4418-948c-de4806e63343", "type": "concept", "title": "Research questions"}, {"id": "f3efebc2-5ced-41a3-97d8-575fe258db14", "type": "concept", "title": "Working thesis"}, {"id": "3ba6b3dd-0742-4fc4-956d-c05295398920", "type": "concept", "title": "Annotated bibliography"}], "description": "Students learn to move from curiosity to a focused, answerable, interpretive question."}, {"id": "8db2367e-0caf-42b4-a5f1-dc8b0c4a47e7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sources and evidence", "children": [{"id": "05374e84-1fdb-4a52-8615-59b4ba4a0ee5", "type": "concept", "title": "Primary texts, objects, images, and recordings"}, {"id": "624b7bab-5bf8-4293-813a-82b7ce5a5fd5", "type": "concept", "title": "Scholarly books and articles"}, {"id": "6571463a-9a58-4421-9fec-1b8a12da9199", "type": "concept", "title": "Reference works and research guides"}, {"id": "9b6e8ebd-7121-4a37-93a4-e50bb12ace77", "type": "concept", "title": "Digital archives and databases"}], "description": "Students practice distinguishing primary, secondary, and reference sources and using each appropriately."}, {"id": "f729e97f-f41c-443d-bc14-a062f2c4990e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ethics, citation, and academic integrity", "children": [{"id": "a8994da4-7c00-4437-a18a-83787d2db28b", "type": "concept", "title": "Quotation, paraphrase, and summary"}, {"id": "3d9f097b-170b-449c-881b-07950498df08", "type": "concept", "title": "MLA and Chicago basics"}, {"id": "1edc1d7a-67bb-4797-9f6d-f5e2863025a7", "type": "concept", "title": "Plagiarism and patchwriting"}, {"id": "14293d0f-7842-4cad-a725-d93eaaafb0a5", "type": "concept", "title": "Cultural sensitivity and respectful use"}], "description": "The point is to understand citation as participation in a community of knowledge, not a formatting chore."}]}, {"id": "c3724dba-d20c-47db-a2a2-6da63eea5d22", "type": "topic", "title": "Capstone Humanistic Interpretation: Curate, Present, Reflect, and Connect to Modern Life", "description": "The capstone asks students to synthesize the course by interpreting a humanities object or question for an audience. They build a polished project that demonstrates evidence, context, argument, reflection, and public communication.", "children": [{"id": "ade715e6-893b-4798-8161-c73756047e38", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Project proposal and object selection", "children": [{"id": "ec458bdd-332c-4d30-947b-7ad6bc2083da", "type": "concept", "title": "Object or question rationale"}, {"id": "c31e55bb-ef4d-4e01-b062-5aaf55213b5c", "type": "concept", "title": "Research plan"}, {"id": "ac14fd71-27b2-4cca-ac61-a8ecf87941b7", "type": "concept", "title": "Audience and purpose"}, {"id": "8ead38ec-b385-46ff-a2d0-2205e5254f45", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethical considerations"}], "description": "Students choose a text, artwork, performance, place, artifact, idea, or cultural debate that rewards humanistic analysis."}, {"id": "b2c2796c-d432-4feb-b5c5-07b3f060cc15", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Interpretive project design", "children": [{"id": "3fbdd12e-438f-466b-bbfa-b41dc60c9d70", "type": "concept", "title": "Argument and structure"}, {"id": "f26ed83e-6ea8-4d70-86e9-bfeca2ce707f", "type": "concept", "title": "Multimodal evidence"}, {"id": "b0a980a8-a1dc-4786-b336-f1c130fa8bae", "type": "concept", "title": "Visual and textual clarity"}, {"id": "428f813d-fc09-40bb-86f3-c18b1b52ff44", "type": "concept", "title": "Accessibility and citation"}], "description": "Students translate research into a clear form such as an essay, exhibit, podcast, presentation, digital exhibit, or public-facing guide."}, {"id": "e0888d3b-4ac5-4948-bfee-f842134f4828", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reflection and transfer", "children": [{"id": "bfac4289-809f-4523-ab79-57e9491949c0", "type": "concept", "title": "Process reflection"}, {"id": "5cfb51c8-8ddc-4002-8eca-4ade36826cfe", "type": "concept", "title": "Peer critique and revision"}, {"id": "9757b5ed-ca32-4962-9d3b-cf6db9ecad30", "type": "concept", "title": "Connection to modern life"}, {"id": "d7ed17fb-49e6-4e28-9619-be8890d199da", "type": "concept", "title": "Portfolio-ready documentation"}], "description": "The final step asks students to articulate what humanistic inquiry changes in how they read, see, listen, judge, and participate in public life."}]}], "dbBacked": true}$ihumjson$::jsonb,
